+- **io_uring for socket writes, one plain `recvfrom` per read.** io-event picks io_uring on the
+  droplet's kernel. Writes go through the fiber scheduler, so io_uring submits every worker's
+  response in one batch instead of one syscall each. Together with edge-triggered epoll, that cut
+  CPU at 40k users from about 46% to 40% on our test VM. Reads don't use the ring: a connection
+  reaches a worker only after an epoll edge said it is readable, so each read is one
+  `read_nonblock` into the worker's reusable 8 KB String, which on Ruby 4.0.7 is a single
+  `recvfrom(MSG_DONTWAIT)` with no `fcntl`, no io_uring submission, no fiber transfer back to the
+  reactor and no extra reactor pass per batch. An earlier version submitted the first read of each
+  connection through the scheduler's `io_read` with an `IO::Buffer`; that was an SQE, an
+  `io_uring_enter`, a CQE and two fiber switches per request, and paired runs at 20k users measured
+  about 1.3% less CPU per request without it. Because that read waited for data, a worker that
+  picked up a stale edge was parked until the client sent again; `read_nonblock` returns
+  `:wait_readable` instead and the worker goes straight back to the pool.
